<%# Which kinds of set people spot fast, and which they miss. Every figure in the text and every chart comes from one saved copy of the /stats numbers (SpottingSets), so they agree with each other; /stats has the live ones. %>
<% post = SpottingSets.current %>
<% url = "#{ApplicationHelper::SITE_URL}#{Blog.path(SpottingSets)}" %>
<% site = "#{ApplicationHelper::SITE_URL}/" %>
<% content_for :title, SpottingSets::TITLE %>
<% content_for :social_title, SpottingSets::TITLE %>
<% content_for :description, SpottingSets::DESCRIPTION %>
<% content_for :og_type, "article" %>
<% content_for :site_page, "blog" %>
<% content_for :head, render("blog/styles") %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <script type="application/ld+json"><%= raw json_escape({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    headline: SpottingSets::TITLE,
    description: SpottingSets::DESCRIPTION,
    url: url,
    mainEntityOfPage: url,
    datePublished: SpottingSets::PUBLISHED_ON.iso8601,
    inLanguage: "en",
    publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: ApplicationHelper::SITE_NAME, url: site },
    isPartOf: { "@type": "WebSite", name: ApplicationHelper::SITE_NAME, url: site }
  }.to_json) %></script>
<% end %>
<% as_of = post_date(post.taken_at) %>
<% easiest, hardest = post.kinds.first, post.kinds.last %>
<% per_set = post.finds_per_set %>
<% fit = post.multiplicative_fit %>
<% shadings = post.quickest_shadings %>
<%# What a set's three cards have in common, the two features the eye seems to catch first leading. %>
<% glance = %w[color shading] %>
<% shared = ->(set) { (glance | SetStats::ATTRS) - post.differing(set[:cards]) } %>
<% same_glance_quickest = post.quickest.count { |set| (glance - shared.(set)).empty? } %>
<% all_four_least = post.least_found.count { |set| post.differing(set[:cards]).length == SetStats::ATTRS.length } %>
<% least_exceptions = post.least_found.reject { |set| post.differing(set[:cards]).length == SetStats::ATTRS.length } %>
<% shape_only, number_only = secs(post.median_ms("all but shape")), secs(post.median_ms("all but number")) %>
<%= render "shared/card_symbols" %>
<main class="blog">
  <article>
    <header class="top">
      <p class="crumb"><a href="/blog">Blog</a></p>
      <h1><%= SpottingSets::TITLE %></h1>
      <p class="deck"><%= num(post.timed_finds) %> timed finds on Set Daily show how each differing feature multiplies search time, and why sharing color alone beats sharing shape and number.</p>
      <p class="dateline"><time datetime="<%= SpottingSets::PUBLISHED_ON.iso8601 %>"><%= post_date(SpottingSets::PUBLISHED_ON) %></time> · Numbers as of <time datetime="<%= post.taken_at.iso8601 %>"><%= as_of %></time></p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <figure class="versus">
        <div class="card">
          <% [easiest, hardest].each do |kind| %>
            <div class="side">
              <%= set_cards(kind[:cards]) %>
              <p class="time"><b><%= secs(kind[:median_ms]) %></b> <span><%= differing_phrase(post.differing(kind[:cards])) %></span></p>
              <span class="len" aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:<%= (kind[:median_ms] * 100.0 / hardest[:median_ms]).round(1) %>%"></i></span>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Two sets, and the median time players take to find one of each kind.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Look at the two sets above. Even if you have never played Set, the first reads as a group at a glance, while the second looks like three unrelated cards until you check each feature one by one.
        Across <%= num(post.timed_finds) %> timed finds on Set Daily, sets like the first take a median of <%= secs(easiest[:median_ms]) %>; sets like the second take <%= secs(hardest[:median_ms]) %>, <%= number_word(post.slowdown) %> times as long.
      </p>
      <p>
        The rule of Set: three cards make a set when each of four features (number, color, shape, shading) is either the same on all three cards or different on all three.
        A set can differ in one, two, three or all four features, giving <%= post.kinds.length %> kinds in all (2<sup>4</sup> − 1).
      </p>
      <p>
        Set Daily logs how long each find takes in its Daily Deal and Practice games, anonymously: <%= num(post.finds) %> sets found by <%= num(post.players) %> players so far, <%= num(post.timed_finds) %> of them with clean inter-find times (excluding gaps over <%= number_word(SetStats::IDLE_MS / 60_000) %> minutes).
        Here are all <%= post.kinds.length %> kinds, fastest first.
      </p>

      <figure class="wide">
        <%= render "stats/how_fast", kinds: post.kinds %>
        <figcaption>Median time to find a set, by which features are all different, with one example of each kind. Bar: the middle half of finds (25th to 75th percentile). Line: the median. Numbers as of <%= as_of %>; <a href="/stats">the stats page</a> has them live.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Every difference multiplies the search</h2>
      <p>Group the <%= post.kinds.length %> kinds by how many features differ, and the medians climb like stairs.</p>
      <dl class="card stairs">
        <% (1..4).each do |differences| %>
          <div>
            <dt><span class="dots" aria-hidden="true"><% 4.times do |dot| %><i class="<%= "on" if dot < differences %>"></i><% end %></span><%= differences %> different</dt>
            <dd><%= seconds_range(post.median_range_ms(differences)) %>s</dd>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </dl>
      <p>
        Look closer and the climb is multiplicative, not additive.
        Fitting one multiplier per differing feature across all <%= post.kinds.length %> medians explains <%= format("%.1f%%", fit[:r_squared] * 100) %> of the variance (R<sup>2</sup> = <%= format("%.3f", fit[:r_squared]) %>, from a <%= secs(fit[:base_ms]) %> baseline): making color differ multiplies find time by <%= format("%.2f×", fit[:multipliers]["color"]) %>, shading by <%= format("%.2f×", fit[:multipliers]["shading"]) %>, shape by <%= format("%.2f×", fit[:multipliers]["shape"]) %>, and number by <%= format("%.2f×", fit[:multipliers]["number"]) %>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That multiplicative pattern fits how you scan a 12-card board.
        When three cards share a feature your eye can filter on, you ignore roughly two-thirds of the board and search only the cards that match.
        Losing a shared filter does not add a fixed couple of seconds; it multiplies the candidate cards you have to inspect.
        The slow tail stretches out the same way: for the easiest kind, three quarters of finds finish within <%= secs(easiest[:p75_ms]) %>, while a quarter of “all different” finds drag past <%= secs(hardest[:p75_ms]) %>.
      </p>

      <h2>Color and shading do the work</h2>
      <p>
        The steps in the stairs above overlap (two differences run <%= seconds_range(post.median_range_ms(2)) %>s, three run <%= seconds_range(post.median_range_ms(3)) %>s) because <em>which</em> features differ matters as much as how many.
        Turning the chart around so each kind is named for what its three cards share makes the hierarchy jump out.
      </p>

      <figure class="shared">
        <div class="card">
          <% { 2 => "Two features shared", 1 => "One feature shared" }.each do |count, heading| %>
            <h3><%= heading %></h3>
            <ol role="list">
              <% post.kinds.select { |kind| shared.(kind).length == count }.each do |kind| %>
                <li>
                  <span class="chips"><span class="sr">Same </span><% shared.(kind).each do |feature| %><span class="<%= "strong" if glance.include?(feature) %>"><%= feature %></span> <% end %></span>
                  <%= set_cards(kind[:cards]) %>
                  <span class="len" aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:<%= (kind[:median_ms] * 100.0 / hardest[:median_ms]).round(1) %>%"></i></span>
                  <b><%= secs(kind[:median_ms]) %></b>
                </li>
              <% end %>
            </ol>
          <% end %>
        </div>
        <figcaption>The two- and three-difference kinds from the chart above, named for the features the three cards have in common, with the median time to find each. Highlighted: color and shading.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        With two features shared, color and shading together are the fastest pair at <%= secs(post.median_ms("number + shape")) %>, and number and shape together are the slowest at <%= secs(post.median_ms("color + shading")) %>.
        The four pairs in the middle, each pairing color or shading with shape or number, sit within a second of each other and more than a second ahead of sharing neither.
      </p>
      <p>
        The sharpest crossover sits across the two- and three-difference boundary: sharing color alone (<%= secs(post.median_ms("all but color")) %>) beats sharing both number and shape (<%= secs(post.median_ms("color + shading")) %>) by over half a second, and sharing shading alone (<%= secs(post.median_ms("all but shading")) %>) stays within a second of them.
        Shape and number on their own help much less: <%= shape_only == number_only ? "#{shape_only} each" : "#{shape_only} and #{number_only}" %>, against <%= secs(post.median_ms("all four")) %> for sharing nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        This matches visual-search research (Treisman’s feature integration theory): color and fill density are preattentive features that the eye groups in parallel across a board, while matching shapes and counts takes slower, serial scrutiny.
      </p>

      <h2 id="h-skipped">The hard ones get left on the board</h2>
      <p>
        Any two cards in Set uniquely force the third, so an 81-card deck contains exactly <%= num(SetStats::TOTAL_SETS) %> possible sets (81 × 80 / 6).
        Counting them by how many features differ yields exact round shares: <%= SetStats::CHANCE[1] %>% with one difference (<%= post.possible_sets_by_diff(1) %> sets), <%= SetStats::CHANCE[2] %>% with two (<%= post.possible_sets_by_diff(2) %>), <%= SetStats::CHANCE[3] %>% with three (<%= post.possible_sets_by_diff(3) %>), and <%= SetStats::CHANCE[4] %>% with all four (<%= post.possible_sets_by_diff(4) %>).
      </p>
      <p>
        In the deck, all-different sets are twice as common as one-difference sets.
        In play, they are found at nearly the same rate: <%= format("%.1f%%", post.shares.fetch(4)) %> of finds against <%= format("%.1f%%", post.shares.fetch(1)) %>.
      </p>

      <figure>
        <%= render "stats/what_gets_found", shares: post.shares, labelledby: "h-skipped" %>
        <figcaption>Share of finds by how many features are all different. Tick: the share among all <%= num(SetStats::TOTAL_SETS) %> possible sets. Numbers as of <%= as_of %>.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Divided by how many sets of each kind exist, an average one-difference set is found <%= format("%.1f", post.avg_finds_per_set_by_diff(1)) %> times in the timed data, while an average all-different set is found <%= format("%.1f", post.avg_finds_per_set_by_diff(4)) %> times, roughly half as often.
        Per-kind find rates mirror the speed ranking too: sharing color and shading (<%= format("%.2f×", post.relative_rate("number + shape")) %> deck odds) beats one-difference shading (<%= format("%.2f×", post.relative_rate("shading")) %>), and sharing color alone (<%= format("%.2f×", post.relative_rate("all but color")) %>) is still found more often than chance even with three differences.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both results, <%= number_word(post.slowdown) %> times slower and heavily under-represented, come from the same race on the board.
        A 12-card board often holds more than one set, and two sets can share a card.
        Because players scan for easy, same-color or same-shading sets first, the easy set wins the race in a few seconds and removes a card that breaks the overlapping hard set.
        When a player does claim an all-different set after <%= secs(hardest[:median_ms]) %>, that clock includes both the difficulty of spotting it and the seconds spent ruling out easier sets first.
      </p>

      <h2>The quickest and the rarest</h2>
      <p>
        Two raw lists from the stats page make the same point with no grouping at all: the <%= number_word(post.quickest.length) %> individual sets with the lowest median time, and the <%= number_word(post.least_found.length) %> found least often.
        Each of the rarest sets has been found only <%= post.least_found.map { |set| set[:n] }.minmax.uniq.join(" to ") %> times, compared with <%= per_set[:median] %> for the median set and <%= per_set[:max] %> for the most popular.
        (Part of that wide spread comes from the Daily Deal, where everyone plays the same shuffle, so sets dealt on busy days are found dozens of times at once.)
      </p>

      <figure class="wide pair">
        <section aria-labelledby="h-quickest">
          <h3 id="h-quickest">Quickest to spot</h3>
          <ol class="card sets" role="list">
            <% post.quickest.each_with_index do |set, index| %>
              <li><span class="rk" aria-hidden="true"><%= index + 1 %></span><%= set_cards(set[:cards]) %><span class="val"><%= secs(set[:median_ms]) %><small><%= pluralize(num(set[:n]), "find") %></small></span></li>
            <% end %>
          </ol>
        </section>
        <section aria-labelledby="h-least">
          <h3 id="h-least">Least found</h3>
          <ol class="card sets" role="list">
            <% post.least_found.each_with_index do |set, index| %>
              <li><span class="rk" aria-hidden="true"><%= index + 1 %></span><%= set_cards(set[:cards]) %><span class="val"><%= num(set[:n]) %>×</span></li>
            <% end %>
          </ol>
        </section>
        <figcaption>Quickest to spot: the median time to find that exact set, and the number of finds behind it. Least found: how many times that exact set has been found. Numbers as of <%= as_of %>.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        All <%= number_word(post.quickest.length) %> of the quickest sets differ in a single feature<% if same_glance_quickest == post.quickest.length %> and share both color and shading<% else %>, and in <%= number_word(same_glance_quickest) %> of them the three cards share both color and shading<% end %>.
        <%= number_word(shadings["solid"]).capitalize %> are solid, <%= number_word(shadings["striped"]) %> are striped, and <%= shadings["open"].zero? ? "none are" : "#{number_word(shadings["open"])} #{shadings["open"] == 1 ? "is" : "are"}" %> open.
        <% if least_exceptions.empty? %>
          All <%= number_word(post.least_found.length) %> of the least found differ in all four features.
        <% elsif least_exceptions.length == 1 && shared.(least_exceptions.first).length == 1 %>
          <%= number_word(all_four_least).capitalize %> of the <%= number_word(post.least_found.length) %> least found differ in all four features, and the lone exception shares only <%= shared.(least_exceptions.first).first %>.
        <% else %>
          <%= number_word(all_four_least).capitalize %> of the <%= number_word(post.least_found.length) %> least found differ in all four features.
        <% end %>
      </p>

      <figure class="fact">
        <div class="card">
          <p>The fastest single find so far <b><%= secs(post.fastest_find[:ms]) %></b></p>
          <%= set_cards(post.fastest_find[:cards]) %>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Almost certainly pre-spotted: strong players often line up a second set before claiming the first. Finished runs are replay-verified server-side.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section class="notes" aria-labelledby="h-notes">
        <h2 id="h-notes">How this was counted</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The numbers cover Set Daily’s Daily Deal and Practice games. Finds in the daily Puzzle are not in them: the Puzzle keeps the same 12 cards on screen until all six sets are found, rather than dealing through an 81-card deck.</li>
          <li>A find’s time runs from the previous find, or from the deal, to the moment the set is taken. Every completed game is replayed move by move from its seed on the server (with wall-clock checks and a <%= SoloReplay::Verifier::MIN_CLAIM_GAP_MS %> ms minimum gap between claims) before being saved.</li>
          <li><%= num(post.finds) %> sets were found in all, and <%= num(post.timed_finds) %> of them have a time. The other <%= num(post.untimed_finds) %> either came more than <%= number_word(SetStats::IDLE_MS / 60_000) %> minutes after the previous find, most likely a tab left open, or were in unfinished games that kept only a count.</li>
          <li>This is not a controlled experiment. The players are whoever showed up, and regulars play a lot: the <%= number_word(post.most_games.length) %> most active named players finished <%= num(post.most_games.sum) %> of the <%= num(post.games) %> completed games.</li>
          <li>Everything here was frozen on <%= as_of %>. <a href="/stats">The stats page</a> has the live numbers.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <aside class="card play">
        <p>Set Daily has a new <a href="/">Puzzle</a> every day (12 cards hiding exactly 6 sets) and a <a href="/daily">Daily Deal</a> everyone plays from the same cards.</p>
        <p class="more"><a class="btn" href="/">Play today’s Puzzle</a><a class="btn ghost" href="/daily">Play the Daily Deal</a></p>
      </aside>
      <p class="back"><a href="/blog">← All posts</a></p>
    </div>
  </article>
</main>
